      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've noticed a lot of the shards in this specific layer are very fragmented and the colors are quite inconsistent across the assemblage, which makes it hard to tell if it was a single firing event or multiple small batches. Are there any specific indicators in the slip or the way the surface has weathered that might point to the kiln temperature being unstable during the process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Inconsistent colors and fragmentation, especially when dealing with ancient pottery, can certainly suggest kiln instability. Look for areas of uneven color saturation or \"blotching\" on the surface, as this often points to fluctuating temperatures or inconsistent gas flow within the kiln. If the slip itself shows areas where it has blistered or cracked unevenly, that can be a sign of thermal shock or inconsistent heat application. These variations are a classic indicator of a less controlled firing environment, which is very common in early production settings.",
